{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And to the children of Israel you shall speak, saying.</b> Why Moses commanded Aaron to speak to Israel and did not command them himself — it is because Moses wished to show all Israel that Aaron was clean of the sin of the calf, for were it not so, how could he command them: 'Take for yourselves a calf for atonement'? — would they not say to him: 'Remove the beam from your own eye,' and so forth. Or the meaning may be along the lines of 'Adorn yourself first,' and so forth (Bava Batra 60a). Therefore first 'Take for yourself a calf, a young bull' — to adorn yourself first, and afterward 'And to the children of Israel you shall speak, saying' — that they too should adorn themselves, and then they too can adorn others. And this is the meaning of the word לֵאמֹר, 'saying.'" "<b>Take a goat for a sin-offering.</b> For atonement over the fact that they slaughtered their sacrifices to the goat-demons (se'irim); therefore it says 'to slaughter before YHWH' — and not to the goat-demons, as they did in the slaughter of the calf, as mentioned. And in Torat Kohanim (Leviticus 9:3) they expounded: What did Israel see to bring more than Aaron? Rather, Moses said to them: You have something from the beginning and you have something from the end — at the beginning, as it says (Genesis 37:31), 'And they slaughtered a goat'; at the end, as it says (Exodus 32:8), 'They made themselves a molten calf.' Let a goat come and atone for the deed of the goat; let a calf come and atone for the deed of the calf. But Aaron had a share only in the calf — end of quotation. And one must scrutinize: why did he remove Aaron from the category of the deed of the goat? For Levi too was involved in that deed, and what is more, he had a great share in that deed more than his brothers, as Rashi explained on the verse 'Simeon and Levi are brothers' (Genesis 49:5). Furthermore: why did he mention the language of 'beginning' and 'end'? He should have said: you have two transgressions in your hands — why did he hang the sin on what is prior and what is later? Furthermore: what is the connection between the atonement of a goat and the atonement of the sin of the calf? Also, the verse 'And all the congregation drew near and stood before YHWH' requires explanation, for it does not specify where they drew near to. And what is the meaning of what it says, 'This is the thing that YHWH has commanded you to do' — what more should they do beyond what they had already done?" "And what seems close to me, to resolve these difficulties, is that the author of this midrash holds that this depends on that — that the deed of 'And they slaughtered a goat' was also a cause for the deed of the calf. For it is well known that the trait of jealousy that was in Joseph's brothers caused them that deed of 'And they slaughtered a goat,' and from then on the trait of jealousy took root among them for generations, as it is written (Psalms 69:10), 'For the jealousy of Your house has consumed me' — that is the jealousy of the house of Israel, prevalent among Israel in all generations more than among all the nations, until this calf emerged from the midst of many who were jealous of Moses in the camp, like Korah and his congregation. And likewise at that time many jealous ones assailed him, and this is the way of jealousies: to contrive false charges against him, saying 'For this Moses the man, we do not know what has become of him' (Exodus 32:1), in order to cast off his yoke from upon them and to set over themselves this calf — the despised idol gripped by cords of contempt. For the jealousy that brings about separation of hearts is what stood them in stead, that each man separated from his brother, for they nullified the commandment of 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself.' And this is the prior sin that brings about also the later one, that in the end they turned the back of the neck also toward YHWH and nullified the commandment of 'And you shall love YHWH your Elohim.' And for this reason our Sages of blessed memory said (Bereishit Rabbah 38:6) that as long as there is peace among Israel, even if they serve idolatry, leave them be, as it says (Hosea 4:17), 'Ephraim is joined to idols — leave him be'; but 'Their heart is divided — now they shall bear guilt.' And the explanation of this matter you will find well and properly set forth in my work Orelot Ephraim, part 1, discourse 23, and from there you can understand why the author of this midrash hung these two kinds of sin on the prior and the later — for one draws the other in its wake. And so we find (I Kings 12:28) with Jeroboam, that he made the calves out of his jealousy of the kingdom of Solomon." "And with this premise, it is settled why Aaron did not need to have a share in the atonement that concerns the prior sin, 'And they slaughtered a goat' — that is, the separation of hearts that existed among the Patriarchs — for Aaron had already repaired this through his embrace of the trait of peace, as it says, 'To obey is better than sacrifice' (I Samuel 15:22). And so Israel undertook to repair both on this day. This is what it says: 'And all the congregation drew near,' and so forth. For when all the congregation saw that they needed atonement for 'And they slaughtered a goat,' which jealousy and separation of hearts had caused them, and for the later one, which is the deed of the calf, through which they distanced themselves from YHWH and turned the back of the neck and not the face toward Him — behold, besides the sacrifice, they saw fit to repair all of this in actual deed, that it should not be like one who immerses while holding a creeping thing in his hand. And this is what it says: 'And they took all that Moses had commanded' — that is, the sacrifices — 'and all the congregation drew near' — that they drew near, this one to that one, in the drawing near of the hearts. And they undertook and accepted upon themselves to be in brotherhood and friendship, to repair the prior sin, the deed of the goat, which comes from separation of hearts. And afterward they also repaired the later sin: 'and they stood before YHWH' — for they turned toward HaShem, blessed be He, face and not the back of the neck. And when Moses saw the fitness of their hearts with one another, and that they were whole with YHWH, the matter was very pleasing in his eyes, for it is better than the bringing of a sacrifice, as it says (I Samuel 15:22), 'Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice.' And concerning this Moses said: 'This is the thing that YHWH has commanded you to do' — as if HaShem had commanded nothing concerning burnt-offerings and sacrifice but only to obey the voice of YHWH. For by this matter, the performance of the sacrifices is diminished — to say: you shall do likewise for all generations. And would that this were your heart all the days! For through its merit 'the glory of YHWH shall appear to you' — just as you turn your faces toward the Shekhinah, so may YHWH illuminate His face toward you. In the manner that He comes to see, so He comes to be seen."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase טוֹל קוֹרָה ('take a beam') is an ellipsis of the Talmudic idiom טוֹל קורה מבין עיניך ('remove the beam from between your eyes'); the abbreviation כוּ׳ signals the elision."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:51.319775928Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}